        "page": 1,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p1_s2"
      },
      "text": "In this article we're going to analyze the nature of the attacks, the functionality of the backdoor - here labelled as ByeBye Shell - and the quick interaction I had with the operators behind this campaign.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_9",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "ByeBye_Shell_target",
      "locator": {
        "page": 4,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p4_s1"
      },
      "text": "Again, the original article is available on Dawn.com .",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_11",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "ByeBye_Shell_target",
      "locator": {
        "page": 5,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p5_s2"
      },
      "text": "PoisonIvy, Gh0st and custom backdoors are daily business for threat analysts and malware researchers, in most cases being tedious work with little technical challenge.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_13",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "ByeBye_Shell_target",
      "locator": {
        "page": 5,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p5_s4"
      },
      "text": "The main backdoor installed and executed on the victims' systems appears to be a custom reverse shell with just a handful of features.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_16",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "ByeBye_Shell_target",
      "locator": {
        "page": 5,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p5_s7"
      },
      "text": "Address 0: 10.0.2.15",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_20",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "ByeBye_Shell_target",
      "locator": {
        "page": 8,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p8_s1"
      },
      "text": "In the following screenshot you can see how the reverse shell is implemented: it just launches a cmd.exe and pipes stdin, stdout and stderr to the opened socket so that the operator can directly interact with the Windows prompt.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_23",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "ByeBye_Shell_target",
      "locator": {
        "page": 10,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p10_s2"
      },
      "text": "In order to get some fun out of an overall straightforward analysis, I quickly hacked together a Python script that emulates a ByeBye backdoor - following is the code: 01.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_25",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "ByeBye_Shell_target",
      "locator": {
        "page": 12,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p12_s1"
      },
      "text": "As you can see, this script simply tries to emulate the basic functionality of ByeBye: it performs the initial check-in and waits for incoming messages from the operator.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_32",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "ByeBye_Shell_target",
      "locator": {
        "page": 1,
        "section": "rule",
        "sentence_id": "p1_r1"
      },
      "text": "As you can see, it enforces some configuration in the registry in order to hide file extensions and not show hidden folders. Subsequently the malware creates and launches a Cabinet Self-Extractor , which drops two additional executable files: one embedding either a PDF or a Microsoft Office Word document, the other being the actual backdoor. These are the hashes of the original droppers I inspected during this analysis: 8b4224dac114a9b8433913a1977f88b2",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "note": "rule or indicator snippet evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
